\details{
O-information is an information-theoretic measure proposed by
Rosas et al. (2019) and introduced to psychometric hypergraph analysis by
Marinazzo et al. (2024). It quantifies the balance between redundant and
synergistic higher-order statistical dependencies among three or more
variables.

The O-information is defined as:

\deqn{
\Omega = TC - DTC
}

where \eqn{TC} is the Total Correlation and \eqn{DTC} is the
Dual Total Correlation.

Total Correlation (TC) measures the amount of collective constraint
among variables and is defined as:

\deqn{
TC(X_1,\ldots,X_n)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n} H(X_i)
-
H(X_1,\ldots,X_n)
}

where \eqn{H(\cdot)} denotes Shannon entropy.

Dual Total Correlation (DTC) measures the amount of shared randomness
among variables and is defined as:

\deqn{
DTC(X_1,\ldots,X_n)
=
H(X_1,\ldots,X_n)
-
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
H(X_i \mid X_{-i})
}

where \eqn{X_{-i}} denotes all variables except \eqn{X_i}.

For multivariate Gaussian variables, TC and DTC can be computed directly
from the covariance (or correlation) matrix. Given a correlation matrix
\eqn{\Sigma} and precision matrix \eqn{C=\Sigma^{-1}},

\deqn{
TC = -\frac{1}{2}\log |\Sigma|
}

and

\deqn{
DTC =
\frac{1}{2}
\left(
\log |\Sigma|
+
\sum_i \log C_{ii}
\right).
}

Interpretation of \eqn{\Omega}:

\itemize{
\item \eqn{\Omega > 0}: redundancy-dominated interaction
("redundant"), indicating that the system is dominated by low-order
constraints.
\item \eqn{\Omega < 0}: synergy-dominated interaction
("synergistic"), indicating a predominance of higher-order constraints
within the system of interest.
\item \eqn{\Omega \approx 0}: balanced or neutral interaction.
}

In psychometric hypergraphs, subsets with positive O-information are
represented as redundant hyperedges, whereas subsets with negative
O-information are represented as synergistic hyperedges.

This implementation follows the Gaussian formulation described in
